# Ruby & Rails Conventions

## Ruby Version
- Target Ruby **<%= ruby_version %>**
- Use modern Ruby syntax (pattern matching, endless methods where readable)

## Rails Version
- Target Rails **<%= rails_version %>**
- Follow Rails conventions (Convention over Configuration)

## Database
<%- if postgresql? -%>
- PostgreSQL — use `jsonb` columns, array columns, and native types where appropriate
- Prefer `citext` for case-insensitive text
<%- elsif mysql? -%>
- MySQL — use `utf8mb4` charset for all string/text columns
- Avoid JSON columns for queried data; prefer normalized tables
<%- else -%>
- SQLite3 — keep queries simple, avoid database-specific features
<%- end -%>

<%- if active_job_backend -%>
## Background Jobs
- Active Job backend: **<%= active_job_backend %>**
- Keep jobs idempotent and retriable
- Set appropriate queue names and priorities
<%- end -%>

## Code Style
- Follow RuboCop rules (`.rubocop.yml`)
- Max line length: 120 characters
- Use `frozen_string_literal: true` in all Ruby files
- Prefer `&&` / `||` over `and` / `or`

## Naming
- Models: singular (`User`, `OrderItem`)
- Controllers: plural (`UsersController`)
- Database tables: plural snake_case (`order_items`)
- Methods/variables: snake_case
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Classes/Modules: CamelCase

## Testing
- RSpec for all tests
- Factory Bot for test data
- Organize specs mirroring `app/` structure
- Use `let` and `subject` for setup, `it`/`context`/`describe` for structure
